[{:db/id "q-86724/selection/09074d944e366ffa3d909641449040bfc0cf5cbf14a228373bcb4fac64a62d68" :answer/id #uuid "947b0d23-fa0c-5331-a127-af74f449a7e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86724/selection/b0fae572d48614a37994e28adcd91c296fe6d6b652511a0af8fe5f205654d6a2" :answer/id #uuid "56e537f9-e65d-55b0-aa88-09458fb6296f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86724/selection/afae609d80363c147107c378839f5932b6aa83b5ada09fd0fe18bb5a60ba918d" :answer/id #uuid "7abb76ea-9dbf-5d19-92d7-a87bdee1bdc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86724/selection/4613ed70822021d53d18cb81e433c09a05771d8e1156223f924c0b82119c8d55" :answer/id #uuid "437d3f49-0cb0-53b2-b38f-81d830b39935" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86724/selection/2aa48fe82edf93230b8946896f4da64f15b2f4849272db6c0d27b650bfcd65ed" :answer/id #uuid "283b2846-e1ce-562b-b021-9810c3b73d37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86724/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f5a469-864e-5d3f-b60f-cce30f250956" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86724/selection/4613ed70822021d53d18cb81e433c09a05771d8e1156223f924c0b82119c8d55" :answer-field/choices ["q-86724/selection/09074d944e366ffa3d909641449040bfc0cf5cbf14a228373bcb4fac64a62d68" "q-86724/selection/b0fae572d48614a37994e28adcd91c296fe6d6b652511a0af8fe5f205654d6a2" "q-86724/selection/afae609d80363c147107c378839f5932b6aa83b5ada09fd0fe18bb5a60ba918d" "q-86724/selection/4613ed70822021d53d18cb81e433c09a05771d8e1156223f924c0b82119c8d55" "q-86724/selection/2aa48fe82edf93230b8946896f4da64f15b2f4849272db6c0d27b650bfcd65ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86724" :question/id #uuid "24be252f-8729-57e6-8082-158ca3588445" :question/math-academy-id "q-86724" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region  $D={(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(3,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-2,0)$, and $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/752c84f5ab0fb122102629e9ceada21c1bba0b8f0e93b3fffe0639e4284badab.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}-6(-2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}-6(2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the point $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-86724/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86724"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "4f91b380-ee58-5fe6-858d-8cf90488d38c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "67cab74c-22de-52ae-b65e-832cd36fc378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8e94a88a-e447-5801-8106-bc61c4413f7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "69d01e94-019d-519a-9815-5fe4a426d0b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "5da01e5e-8c65-5692-88c4-67732d20c5b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd931f2d-03a7-5433-b945-abf6f35b98af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-167160/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-167160/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-167160/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-167160/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-167160/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-167160/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167160" :question/id #uuid "4e592e78-9c13-5052-a90f-fc4932f102d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167160" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+2y-1$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1),$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & ={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =1-0+0-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1-1+2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =0-1+2-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167160/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167160"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709/selection/2363230d06ac594e2e7984d3a705f7070b3b0fcfb583f0458fa3f17115662e57" :answer/id #uuid "3756c219-1da8-5bb4-a294-3dc002175640" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709/selection/0489075ccd4781e59a9040be5b67cee17cf9d7b1b903594c0a16be41a8b63bb4" :answer/id #uuid "6d8b01e4-a20b-561c-8f3f-b9775b5242d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709/selection/7a51d973a1e3c71d95345010c37232b55bc2bd6101be2884f083f23eb7c84277" :answer/id #uuid "90e1e85e-9100-5a81-a0ab-27a2891f537e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709/selection/1711bdfea17393ae46d9e78791a878b630e5972101f53f130606ddb0908cbec8" :answer/id #uuid "4c52d5ee-bca2-51df-833c-5f437b92a5a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709/selection/2c346d9fb2f94b2e74523ee40c3077a5219c64702281fb0a4ec762d517d3e89f" :answer/id #uuid "0a056d3a-6379-5430-9a27-c8c8a6ee0afe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e31d53-0d66-5f58-b848-6c9911036097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86709/selection/2363230d06ac594e2e7984d3a705f7070b3b0fcfb583f0458fa3f17115662e57" :answer-field/choices ["q-86709/selection/2363230d06ac594e2e7984d3a705f7070b3b0fcfb583f0458fa3f17115662e57" "q-86709/selection/0489075ccd4781e59a9040be5b67cee17cf9d7b1b903594c0a16be41a8b63bb4" "q-86709/selection/7a51d973a1e3c71d95345010c37232b55bc2bd6101be2884f083f23eb7c84277" "q-86709/selection/1711bdfea17393ae46d9e78791a878b630e5972101f53f130606ddb0908cbec8" "q-86709/selection/2c346d9fb2f94b2e74523ee40c3077a5219c64702281fb0a4ec762d517d3e89f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86709" :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-86709/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86709"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37369"] :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1){e}^{0}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-1-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1){e}^{1}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e-1-2e \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,1) & =2(0){e}^{1}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =0-0-2e \\\\  & =-2e \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0){e}^{y}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2e$ at the point $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $-1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" :answer/id #uuid "98a57c4c-0a05-5b9c-bd55-7e4764a5914c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725/selection/17036060b584e0bff735f172125b7298aac70cbe5779958ca2cc7366f91c7c5a" :answer/id #uuid "21116642-ce8e-5b84-bea4-3d5b01f796f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725/selection/05213a4e6b78cba67d0a0be71ed0f426f16c767efa740b9c8d2b9e0ae01454aa" :answer/id #uuid "acc7aff4-0fa8-5b3e-98af-f4d2a48966ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt[4]{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725/selection/a6c24763be7bc10534ff181db6f9bbc4ce5918a064f5ae69027c6cc73fedb0f0" :answer/id #uuid "34f83f6d-42e2-5ed5-ae3b-2509daebf501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725/selection/538c8670cb439d1a98a40b4c40205e43f4702337d59966894e4057eb0e28d3b2" :answer/id #uuid "df7c962f-d303-508a-8cce-adc7fab78911" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d51b8917-ea4a-5652-8f39-945a3ee370d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86725/selection/05213a4e6b78cba67d0a0be71ed0f426f16c767efa740b9c8d2b9e0ae01454aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-86725/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" "q-86725/selection/17036060b584e0bff735f172125b7298aac70cbe5779958ca2cc7366f91c7c5a" "q-86725/selection/05213a4e6b78cba67d0a0be71ed0f426f16c767efa740b9c8d2b9e0ae01454aa" "q-86725/selection/a6c24763be7bc10534ff181db6f9bbc4ce5918a064f5ae69027c6cc73fedb0f0" "q-86725/selection/538c8670cb439d1a98a40b4c40205e43f4702337d59966894e4057eb0e28d3b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86725" :question/id #uuid "a5df2c90-a58d-503d-b82e-9a30b33b02be" :question/math-academy-id "q-86725" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy-x}$ on the closed triangular region  $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices and the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/42b9bcda74e29cf7012fd9576ee7bcea92bfc3f58fab4c43c854892afe3acc44.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2{e}^{(0)(0)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2{e}^{(2)(0)-(2)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2} \\\\ f(0,1) & =2{e}^{(0)(1)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,2) & =2{e}^{(0)(2)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =2{e}^{(1/2)(3/2)-(1/2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[4]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2{e}^{-2}$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2\\sqrt[4]{e}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-86725/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86725"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442/selection/5dff1cffe9056e6d634810a93873f3228e636b02a1c4509cdf01fff0c9209c97" :answer/id #uuid "08a26739-be99-5756-8d58-ba90afc81f36" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442/selection/ff11ef21c20797fde2ca878672bba08476814c27e99a6d0985df92b8035f64d8" :answer/id #uuid "3fc5beb9-f85c-5b81-b47f-6341f5540f41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442/selection/33e09e4ac70fe4b3d5ec6fbe90bea895f175d5dd6d49286ad037ea78ac09678e" :answer/id #uuid "f3521c4c-956a-5f31-a692-d43117ec36c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442/selection/0a5a3b41dd6d392b757cb5c053e472951268624282187fc16843f7301703edc5" :answer/id #uuid "ecf1b048-cb21-5fc5-8e19-3bfc0b2b3c17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442/selection/f6262dda0fdc4494d4db30ff9ed574e6513b3b827a4177f167a42497869de2e2" :answer/id #uuid "b92598d0-124e-52f1-b897-1c1d19f2a878" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "85fdcd4c-e636-5667-9b6f-66cfb0b10825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83442/selection/0a5a3b41dd6d392b757cb5c053e472951268624282187fc16843f7301703edc5" :answer-field/choices ["q-83442/selection/5dff1cffe9056e6d634810a93873f3228e636b02a1c4509cdf01fff0c9209c97" "q-83442/selection/ff11ef21c20797fde2ca878672bba08476814c27e99a6d0985df92b8035f64d8" "q-83442/selection/33e09e4ac70fe4b3d5ec6fbe90bea895f175d5dd6d49286ad037ea78ac09678e" "q-83442/selection/0a5a3b41dd6d392b757cb5c053e472951268624282187fc16843f7301703edc5" "q-83442/selection/f6262dda0fdc4494d4db30ff9ed574e6513b3b827a4177f167a42497869de2e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83442" :question/id #uuid "c7634462-06f5-5c9c-bd25-d298b7d6ce94" :question/math-academy-id "q-83442" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives  \n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points: \n\n$$\n(1,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(-1,0),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,1) & =(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-1,0) & =(-1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,-1) & =(0)^{2}-(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and  $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and $(1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83442/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83442"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722/selection/c5823a9f57ac79b0aad7650330d01086f16a673c5cb9f3756a4e68d6df8acb2a" :answer/id #uuid "de95eb9e-1105-5980-90e9-146f79628bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722/selection/25c2dd989748ff13547c1b50aff8b511fc629a3f9f34a034b3bbd4198106a6e6" :answer/id #uuid "6220656c-5eae-56bc-b67b-6cf3b4f2e528" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722/selection/1819542aabd20b79033235504b0e7212012a7a79579be9b26776ae71b88b4d78" :answer/id #uuid "1fbf8c47-f9b3-5731-bbcf-0c38b19b99f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722/selection/2e6260515b6fa463c6e4c8f727c1947c360e4fe549f6f0a2563bf331dff2a408" :answer/id #uuid "26b28f33-e938-58eb-8dad-3c80b6553b7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722/selection/2d097c6098f80f52ee5ba9435064eca19bf6e687390453c16224718cf248b46c" :answer/id #uuid "ebd002ca-49e0-5e4e-aac7-1e2d6c591983" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff6b916-dbc3-5797-a6d2-7f92135ed397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86722/selection/1819542aabd20b79033235504b0e7212012a7a79579be9b26776ae71b88b4d78" :answer-field/choices ["q-86722/selection/c5823a9f57ac79b0aad7650330d01086f16a673c5cb9f3756a4e68d6df8acb2a" "q-86722/selection/25c2dd989748ff13547c1b50aff8b511fc629a3f9f34a034b3bbd4198106a6e6" "q-86722/selection/1819542aabd20b79033235504b0e7212012a7a79579be9b26776ae71b88b4d78" "q-86722/selection/2e6260515b6fa463c6e4c8f727c1947c360e4fe549f6f0a2563bf331dff2a408" "q-86722/selection/2d097c6098f80f52ee5ba9435064eca19bf6e687390453c16224718cf248b46c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86722" :question/id #uuid "8392959c-a393-5152-9307-9f96b5859277" :question/math-academy-id "q-86722" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-4xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-86722/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86722"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37155"] :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(2,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+4 \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4 \\\\  & =13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4+0-8+4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0-12+4 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0+12+4 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the point $(-3,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165/selection/cef307a89a8638ba785635deb2b60aaf4bb0562a06571adbb4fca9fcc7c61017" :answer/id #uuid "2835875c-80d2-5265-ba35-8fed772aaa4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165/selection/a8f370f6bd0281e264e0bbaddb9587f94f71c87dc8110963894cf0ad477da0c3" :answer/id #uuid "b10078c8-32ac-5aae-aac5-1d3031916af5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165/selection/96fc29d972215babcdde46cd21a9684cb19f8717367e4f62f0916153f533a14c" :answer/id #uuid "5ad1d3c1-942d-5e32-a859-763eb32621f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165/selection/75f7b2b1d69cd7e5a2f3fe54741c6d42b1fcc4cc4aec4077b056ac6cdf0298dd" :answer/id #uuid "d07805de-3f58-5064-ad59-6473a426be5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165/selection/d1dbc1c243355899a0392621f9c6cc2ac2a1254ef06c9574789353d3e85df67b" :answer/id #uuid "2d2d0b09-6d38-5d7c-aadb-97ac0b753014" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "08f924fb-205d-51d9-9fb3-2cffa9ac2b7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-167165/selection/96fc29d972215babcdde46cd21a9684cb19f8717367e4f62f0916153f533a14c" :answer-field/choices ["q-167165/selection/cef307a89a8638ba785635deb2b60aaf4bb0562a06571adbb4fca9fcc7c61017" "q-167165/selection/a8f370f6bd0281e264e0bbaddb9587f94f71c87dc8110963894cf0ad477da0c3" "q-167165/selection/96fc29d972215babcdde46cd21a9684cb19f8717367e4f62f0916153f533a14c" "q-167165/selection/75f7b2b1d69cd7e5a2f3fe54741c6d42b1fcc4cc4aec4077b056ac6cdf0298dd" "q-167165/selection/d1dbc1c243355899a0392621f9c6cc2ac2a1254ef06c9574789353d3e85df67b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167165" :question/id #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167165" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-167165/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167165"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723/selection/b6fd034ecb8b8433626d14aa652c3939f72e11c15831871764bc5dd96e4025d7" :answer/id #uuid "660a7918-4a6b-53d7-a2cc-a1d4acad73df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723/selection/360bcf109d2b1844c2faf7de16fa269d31ba306e5e481c77b59c60c38e1b36bf" :answer/id #uuid "da3ae046-1df8-5da6-b184-ab249db65d77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723/selection/0489075ccd4781e59a9040be5b67cee17cf9d7b1b903594c0a16be41a8b63bb4" :answer/id #uuid "5636d9a9-d54e-501e-9a57-1b60e358df6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723/selection/fddad3546108689aa8136d85f0bf1780d99a43bd05fc3b0ce4f62d144a5e103a" :answer/id #uuid "2a4d0323-ab06-59d3-b315-f17953050c8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723/selection/fc5d288a52e360e39a7c3fc399facb9cbe447a5ad8a52882ab79994265e5855b" :answer/id #uuid "3b56c3bc-64be-5997-832d-f537cbd75266" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4260aba9-d55c-5acf-9432-1f0a37b2f6b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86723/selection/fc5d288a52e360e39a7c3fc399facb9cbe447a5ad8a52882ab79994265e5855b" :answer-field/choices ["q-86723/selection/b6fd034ecb8b8433626d14aa652c3939f72e11c15831871764bc5dd96e4025d7" "q-86723/selection/360bcf109d2b1844c2faf7de16fa269d31ba306e5e481c77b59c60c38e1b36bf" "q-86723/selection/0489075ccd4781e59a9040be5b67cee17cf9d7b1b903594c0a16be41a8b63bb4" "q-86723/selection/fddad3546108689aa8136d85f0bf1780d99a43bd05fc3b0ce4f62d144a5e103a" "q-86723/selection/fc5d288a52e360e39a7c3fc399facb9cbe447a5ad8a52882ab79994265e5855b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86723" :question/id #uuid "ae1c6c82-d3b9-5a35-9dca-71a81e6221fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86723" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-1 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1+0-2-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0-4-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(-2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0+4-1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the point $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-86723/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86723"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "6f695c01-edbb-5ba9-a5af-de59a32345e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "5822f784-fb95-5629-b59a-3c7997d8515e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "19c8a8c5-58ce-5efe-9984-2c8a71f03b9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "c0170195-147f-5c34-b100-9e74cbe5e1f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a6b56e4b-52c6-5319-8367-31f2c6a44856" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "329b0001-9149-55c7-b319-bdca3c4f0648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83474/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-83474/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-83474/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-83474/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-83474/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-83474/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83474" :question/id #uuid "ea81e867-2c7c-56ec-8219-91964c6a140a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83474" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=x-y+1$ on the closed rectangular region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,|x|\\le 1,\\,|y|\\le 1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it is never equal to zero. So, $f$ has no critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary: Notice that $D$ is the square with vertices at $(-1,-1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(1,1),$  and $(-1,1)$ \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,-1) \\\\  & =(t)-(-1)+1 \\\\  & =t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =(1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =2-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,1) \\\\  & =(t)-(1)+1 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAnd finally, \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(-1,t) \\\\  & =(-1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(-1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83474/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83474"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "80356b23-770c-56c0-ab07-db304258d9e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "4b4d4d29-5031-5201-8e0b-7c896f2f5c0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "c46a7516-3fe0-5c0d-8e50-b11bf852ad3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "e94ff72a-cc3d-547c-9d5e-b55e74c442b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "ea37d70b-8112-5d13-99b1-029b0f030b93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4b1198-0ced-5f08-ab64-9e2fcae26a0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83477/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-83477/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-83477/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-83477/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-83477/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-83477/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83477" :question/id #uuid "97e3e680-a9e7-597f-a877-a0daf5245a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-83477" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-1,\\,x-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x-1=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ is not in the interior of $D$,  we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the line $y=2-x,$ in the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t)-(0) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t⟨-2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2-2t,2t) \\\\  & =(2-2t)(2t)-(2-2t)-(2t) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}+4t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-4{t}^{2}+4t-2)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0),\\,(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83477/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83477"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37162"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/5e38a1a4504c2e0f52fe383c948c52032d0bde6eda85d6621d51240cc88a4850.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2xy=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=R.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions ${(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,3]}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(3,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}(t) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(3,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}})}^{2}\\cdot t \\\\  & =9t-{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,3),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9t-{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 9-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(3-{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\sqrt{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,3).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),\\,(3,0),(0,3),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479/selection/86b88b699ba71c821331df893457e1eac71ef0227f79aea0a45dd27def97b076" :answer/id #uuid "4fafbe08-8231-514e-87d0-c77210eed465" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479/selection/8928a7a33f418e63aa54eacd4db77fdebb9a21c449feb2ca4c7e28fa20068c4d" :answer/id #uuid "4c2718ff-4e1d-5cb7-9364-2b22e95d00fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479/selection/5c2372dff6804b804a628dc3acc67bc8483d866df44cb5b89e7a07bb9dcb17b6" :answer/id #uuid "ec24a34d-315b-5b7f-b728-41f56531d029" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479/selection/411d721ac9de1630cb1c1f95f1442d8934446a601b17b4f75a42225dc1144ad4" :answer/id #uuid "20a7758d-64e2-5184-9785-8101bfca3add" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479/selection/03809d8e52a5cebebec9237f75349d60f6bd138d69725798f861b0d5e54f7e11" :answer/id #uuid "516e3828-808a-5428-b5cd-1ed786551382" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-10$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b947942-fe03-5fb8-afd2-4138f12015d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83479/selection/8928a7a33f418e63aa54eacd4db77fdebb9a21c449feb2ca4c7e28fa20068c4d" :answer-field/choices ["q-83479/selection/86b88b699ba71c821331df893457e1eac71ef0227f79aea0a45dd27def97b076" "q-83479/selection/8928a7a33f418e63aa54eacd4db77fdebb9a21c449feb2ca4c7e28fa20068c4d" "q-83479/selection/5c2372dff6804b804a628dc3acc67bc8483d866df44cb5b89e7a07bb9dcb17b6" "q-83479/selection/411d721ac9de1630cb1c1f95f1442d8934446a601b17b4f75a42225dc1144ad4" "q-83479/selection/03809d8e52a5cebebec9237f75349d60f6bd138d69725798f861b0d5e54f7e11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83479" :question/id #uuid "74b4ac53-7bdb-5efd-8618-b21e911d16f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83479" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/8ce943f8a77fe5756fdaf77185d8b59493ef0da95da3605f1f2416786f9ec394.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=xy-4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y,\\,x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0,\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4) \\\\  & =(t)(4)-4 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{2})-4 \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}-4)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(0,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(-2,4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(-2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,4)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(2,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83479/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83479"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4a6e8b75-60b1-5441-97ec-3d64aa68be50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a4836f61-92d9-549a-8e46-037d4d368eca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "edfcfda5-a950-5782-8b3e-ab62b4d9c4d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "5a39b80c-4bdb-5f33-830d-a175c4bd5490" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "8e737a28-23db-5634-8b76-603032d1896b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca18ecc-fd81-578d-9a32-b7d82d20fbf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-167169/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-167169/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-167169/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-167169/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-167169/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-167169/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167169" :question/id #uuid "5afd014f-3b37-56d4-888f-7b1549c6ced7" :question/math-academy-id "q-167169" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=x+y-xy$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-y & 1-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-y=0 \\\\ 1-x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =t+0-t(0) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t⟨1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,t) \\\\  & =t+t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2t-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=1$ does not belong to the range $t\\in (0,1),$ we do not include the corresponding point in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =1+t-t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total, we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-167169/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167169"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12587"] :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2-x-y$ on the closed region ${D}=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^2:\\,0\\leq x\\leq1,\\: x^2\\leq y\\leq 1 \\big\\}.$ \nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0.5,-0.5),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0.5,1),$ $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/a45fd135e45e9b40d06c217e9834923ea4f70809a26e020a8d3900e8fa0a0c22.png)\n\nSince the critical point $\\left(0.5,-0.5\\right)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.   \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n\\left(0.5,1\\right), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.5,1) & ={(0.5)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-0.5-1 \\\\  & =0.25-2.5 \\\\  & =-2.25 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{2}-{0}^{2}-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}-{1}^{2}-0-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2.25$ at the point $\\left(0.5,1\\right),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12588"] :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function  $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-4x$ on the closed region $D=\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4 \\big\\}.$   You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0) \\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince the point $( 2 , 0 )$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step). \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2\\cos{t}, 2\\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution, $t = \\pi \\in (0,2\\pi).$ But we also have to check the endpoint of the interval, $t=0.$ So, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0), \\quad  (-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2) \\\\  & =4+0-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2) \\\\  & =4+0+8 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-4$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(-2,0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12589"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/0ce5cde24ada296d945726d9f8e2f0b85900e62e9891f7ba4e25b6c181b076aa.png)\n\n   \n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-2x+y^2+3$ on the closed region\n${D} = \\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\: : \\: x \\geq 0,\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq4 \\},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol{f}' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/815ebf6434a4b776ff59fb59e5b0783162e9ee69c091ae6d0f59ca94e8cf771b.png)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0).$ Since the point $(1,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 0, t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-2(0)+(t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is inside the interval $t \\in (-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on this boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(0,0).$\n\n- The half-circle connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives the solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in(-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of  $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain a total of four candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (0,-2), \\quad (2,0), \\quad (0,2).\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $7$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(0,-2),$  and $(0,2).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
